<main>

    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Host for friends and family</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">One hub, many vaults. Each person gets their own account; nobody but you holds admin.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What multi-user gives you</h2>

        <p>You run a single Parachute hub on Render, Fly, or your own machine. You create an account per person. Each account is pinned to their own vault. They sign in, change the password you handed them, open Notes, and they're using their own private knowledge graph &mdash; without anyone else able to read or write it.</p>

        <p>Phase 1 is intentionally narrow: <strong>one admin (you), N users, each on one vault.</strong> No self-service signup, no invite links, no multi-vault membership per user, no role granularity below admin/user. Those land later as people ask for them. The point of Phase 1 is to get you hosting your handful of people without standing up infrastructure per person.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>1. Stand up your hub</h2>

        <p>Your hub is the door everyone walks through. It runs as a single container, supervises vault and scribe as child processes, and issues OAuth tokens for clients. Pick the deploy that fits:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><a href="/deploy/render/">Render</a> &mdash; one-click self-host, persistent disk, $7/month flat.</li>
            <li><a href="/deploy/fly/">Fly</a> &mdash; peer to Render, machines + volumes shape, comparable cost.</li>
            <li><a href="/install/">Your own machine</a> &mdash; if you have a home server or VPS, the same hub runs as a daemon.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The wizard at <code>/admin/setup</code> walks you through naming your vault, picking a scribe provider, and exposing your hub. <strong>You</strong> become the first admin during the wizard &mdash; the username and password you pick there are yours.</p>

        <p>Verify before inviting anyone: load your hub URL, sign in, and click around <code>/admin/</code>. If you can see Vaults, Users, Permissions, and Tokens, you're set up correctly.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>2. Provision a vault per friend</h2>

        <p>Every friend gets their own vault. Vaults are private to their assigned user &mdash; the hub mints per-vault scopes, and the OAuth issuer pins each user's tokens to their one vault. There's no path for one user to read another's vault.</p>

        <p>Provision via <code>/admin/vaults</code> &rarr; <strong>Add vault</strong>. Give it a name your friend will recognize when they sign in &mdash; their first name or initials work. <code>maya</code>, <code>jp</code>, <code>dad</code>. Lowercase, alphanumeric, dashes and underscores only.</p>

        <p>The vault boots immediately. Check the vault list shows it as <code>running</code> before moving on.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>3. Create an account for your friend</h2>

        <p>Go to <code>/admin/users</code> &rarr; <strong>Create user</strong>. Three fields:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Username</strong> &mdash; lowercase, 2&ndash;32 characters, alphanumeric + dash + underscore. Your friend will type this every time they sign in; keep it short.</li>
            <li><strong>Default password</strong> &mdash; at least 12 characters. You pick this. Your friend will be force-redirected to change it the moment they sign in.</li>
            <li><strong>Assigned vault</strong> &mdash; pick the vault you just provisioned for them. The dropdown lists every vault on the hub. <em>Pick one.</em> The "No restriction (admin-level access)" option is for promoting someone to co-admin &mdash; not what you want for a friend.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Submit. The user appears in the table with <strong>Password set: No</strong> &mdash; the bit that tells the hub to force-redirect them through change-password on first sign-in.</p>

        <p><strong>Securely hand off the default password.</strong> Signal, an in-person screen-share, a sealed envelope &mdash; not plain email, not a chat log that lives forever. The password is one-time use; the moment your friend signs in they'll be told to pick their own. But it still gates access to their vault until they do, so treat it like the first key to a house.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>4. Send your friend the sign-in link</h2>

        <p>What they need from you:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>Your hub's URL &mdash; e.g. <code>https://parachute.your-domain.com</code> or whatever you set up at the wizard.</li>
            <li>Their username.</li>
            <li>The default password you set.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>A short message that works:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>I set you up with a Parachute vault. Sign in at &lt;hub-url&gt;/login with username &lt;u&gt; and the password I sent separately. You'll be asked to change it on first sign-in. From there, click Open Notes.</code>
        </div>

        <p>When they sign in, they land at <code>/account/change-password</code>, pick a new password, and then are dropped at <code>/account/</code> &mdash; their personal home page. It shows their assigned vault, an <strong>Open Notes</strong> button, and a link to change their password later if they want.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What your friend can and can't do</h2>

        <p><strong>Can:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Open Notes against their assigned vault &mdash; web app at <a href="https://notes.parachute.computer">notes.parachute.computer</a>, runs on any device. Adds itself as a PWA on iOS and Android.</li>
            <li>Connect a custom client to their vault &mdash; a custom Surface running on their laptop, an MCP-aware AI client, or any tool that speaks OAuth + MCP. The hub issues per-vault tokens through the standard OAuth flow; their token is pinned to their vault, can't reach anything else.</li>
            <li>Change their own password from <code>/account/change-password</code> any time.</li>
            <li>Sign out from <code>/account/</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Can't:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>See or read anyone else's vault. The OAuth issuer pins their tokens to their assigned vault name; vault servers enforce scope server-side.</li>
            <li>Reach <code>/admin/</code>. The admin SPA is gated to the first admin (you); anyone else who navigates there gets redirected to <code>/account/</code>.</li>
            <li>Create users, manage vaults, or mint operator-scope tokens. The token-mint endpoints check that the caller is the first admin before issuing.</li>
            <li>Promote themselves or anyone else.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>If a friend wants to build their own Surface</h2>

        <p>This is where multi-user gets interesting. A friend who codes can build their own UI &mdash; a custom Notes-like app, a journaling tool, a research workbench &mdash; that talks directly to their vault on your hub.</p>

        <p>Hand them the <a href="/onboarding/surface-build/">Surface-build starter prompt</a>. It walks an AI client through scaffolding a custom UI that wires into the vault's MCP via the standard OAuth flow. They'll need:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>Your hub's URL (the same one they use to sign in).</li>
            <li>Their vault name (shown on their <code>/account/</code> page).</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The OAuth flow is standard: their app registers as a client via dynamic client registration (DCR), redirects them to your hub for consent, gets a code, exchanges it for a vault-scoped token. <strong>You don't need to pre-register their client.</strong> External clients hit a consent screen on first authorize, where your friend approves &mdash; once they approve, future authorizes are seamless.</p>

        <p>Tokens are pinned to their vault. Their custom Surface can never talk to anyone else's vault, even if they wire it wrong.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>

        <p><strong>Friend forgot their password.</strong> Phase 1 doesn't have self-service reset. The admin recovery shape is "delete the user and re-create them with a fresh default password." Their notes stay in their vault &mdash; vaults are independent from accounts. Delete via <code>/admin/users</code>, re-create with the same username, hand them the new default password.</p>

        <p><strong>Friend's tokens leaked.</strong> Go to <code>/admin/permissions</code> for the per-vault grants view, or <code>/admin/tokens</code> for the per-token registry. Revoke individual tokens or every token for a user. Their OAuth clients will get 401 on next call and re-prompt for consent.</p>

        <p><strong>Wrong vault assigned.</strong> Phase 1 doesn't allow reassigning a user's vault without deleting and re-creating &mdash; that's Phase 2 work. If you need to move a friend to a different vault, delete + re-create. Their notes stay put.</p>

        <p><strong>Removing a friend entirely.</strong> Delete the user via <code>/admin/users</code>. Their vault is <em>not</em> deleted by that action &mdash; vaults and users are independent. If you want the vault gone too, delete it via <code>/admin/vaults</code> after.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>What multi-user is not (yet)</h2>

        <p>Phase 1 is the foundation. Honest about its limits:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>No self-service signup.</strong> You create every account. There's no invite link, no "sign up with email," no SSO.</li>
            <li><strong>No multi-vault membership.</strong> One user, one vault. If your friend wants two vaults (personal + work), they need two accounts.</li>
            <li><strong>No role granularity.</strong> Either you're the admin (first user) or you're a regular user. No "viewer" or "editor" mid-tier.</li>
            <li><strong>No 2FA.</strong> Password-only sign-in. Multi-factor lands later; until then, pick a real password.</li>
            <li><strong>No password reset by user.</strong> Self-service reset is Phase 2; for now, admin deletes + re-creates.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The design document for the full plan is at <a href="/design/2026-05-20-multi-user-phase-1/">/design/2026-05-20-multi-user-phase-1/</a> &mdash; including what Phase 2 and 3 add. If any of the Phase 1 limits block your use case, open an issue at <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-hub/issues">the hub tracker</a> and we'll prioritize.</p>
    </section>

</main>
